r"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">Parabolic trough collectors are used to generate electricity in southern California. (credit: kjkolb, Wikimedia Commons)</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>
</section><section data-depth="1" id="fs-id1167067070336"><h3 data-type="title">The Application of the Curved Mirror Equations</h3>
<div data-type="note" id="fs-id1167067139832" class="os-teacher ui-has-child-title"><header><h3 class="os-title" data-type="title">
  <span class="os-title-label">Teacher Support</span>
</h3></header><section>
<div class="os-note-body"><h4 data-type="title" class="os-subtitle">
  <span class="os-subtitle-label">Teacher Support</span>
</h4>
<p id="fs-id1167063912069"><span>[BL]</span><span>[OL]</span>Review operations for manipulating fractions and for rearranging equations involving fractional values of variables.</p>
<p id="fs-id1167067106376"><span>[AL]</span>Demonstrate how to solve equations of the type  <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow>
 <mrow>
  <mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>a</mi>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>b</mi>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>+</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>c</mi>
  </mfrac>

 </mrow>
</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow>
  <mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>a</mi>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>b</mi>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>+</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>c</mi>
  </mfrac>

 </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>  for any of the variables in terms of the other two. Rearrange so that the variable solved for is not a reciprocal.</p>
</div></section></div>

<p id="fs-id1167067072357">Curved mirrors and the images they create involve a fairly small number of variables: the mirror’s radius of curvature, <em data-effect="italics">R</em>; the focal length, <em data-effect="italics">f</em>; the distances of the object and image from the mirror, <em data-effect="italics">d<sub>o</sub></em> and <em data-effect="italics">d<sub>i</sub></em>, respectively; and the heights of the object and image, <em data-effect="italics">h<sub>o</sub></em> and <em data-effect="italics">h<sub>i</sub></em>, respectively. The signs of these values indicate whether the image is inverted, erect (upright), real, or virtual. We now look at the equations that relate these variables and apply them to everyday problems.</p>
<p id="fs-id1167067123867"><a href="#Figure_16_01_Ray2" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 16.15">Figure 16.15</a> shows the meanings of most of the variables we will use for calculations involving curved mirrors.</p>

<div class="os-figure" id="Figure_16_01_Ray2"><figure data-id="Figure_16_01_Ray2" class="ui-has-child-figcaption"><span data-type="media" id="fs-id1167066822035" data-alt="This figure shows an old-fashioned key inside the focal point of a concave mirror. All the variables used for calculations involving curved mirrors depicted.">
<button type="button" aria-label="Click to enlarge image of This figure shows an old-fashioned key inside the focal point of a concave mirror. All the variables used for calculations involving curved mirrors depicted." class="image-button-wrapper"><img src="/apps/image-cdn/v1/f=webp/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/e66ded20c763520aa5da2250b2176a9ec477560f" data-media-type="image/jpg" alt="This figure shows an old-fashioned key inside the focal point of a concave mirror. All the variables used for calculations involving curved mirrors depicted." width="488" height="253" loading="lazy" data-original-src="/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/e66ded20c763520aa5da2250b2176a9ec477560f"></button>
</span>

<figcaption class="os-caption-container">
  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">16.15</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">Look for the variables, <em data-effect="italics">d<sub>o</sub>, d<sub>i</sub>, h<sub>o</sub>, h<sub>i</sub>,</em> and <em data-effect="italics">f</em> in this figure.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>

<p id="fs-id1167066869482">The basic equation that describes both lenses and mirrors is the lens/mirror equation</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167067144771" class="unnumbered" data-label="">
 <math display="block"><semantics><mrow>
 <mrow>
  <mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>f</mi>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>+</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>.</mo>
 </mrow>
</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow>
  <mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mi>f</mi>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>+</mo><mfrac>
   <mn>1</mn>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>.</mo>
 </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>
</div>
<p id="fs-id1167063733934">This equation can be rearranged several ways. For example, it may be written to solve for focal length.</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167066835708" class="unnumbered" data-label="">
 <math display="block"><semantics><mrow>
 <mrow>
  <mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>
    <mo>+</mo><msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
   </mrow>
</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow>
  <mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>
    <mo>+</mo><msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
   </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>
</div>
<p id="fs-id1167067266273">Magnification, <em data-effect="italics">m</em>, is the ratio of the size of the image, <em data-effect="italics">h<sub>i</sub></em>, to the size of the object, <em data-effect="italics">h<sub>o</sub></em>. The value of <em data-effect="italics">m</em> can be calculated in two ways.</p>

<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167066875321" class="unnumbered" data-label="">
 <math display="block"><semantics><mrow>
 <mrow>
  <mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>h</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>h</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mrow>
    <mo>−</mo><msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
   </mrow>
</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow>
  <mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>h</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>h</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
  <mo>=</mo><mfrac>
   <mrow>
    <mo>−</mo><msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>i</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
   <mrow>
    <msub>
     <mi>d</mi>
     <mi>o</mi>
    </msub>

   </mrow>
  </mfrac>
   </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math>
</div>
<p id="fs-id1167063513588">This relationship can be written to solve for any of the variables involved. For example, the height of the image is given by</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167067139603" class="unnumbered" data-label="">
 <math display="block"><semantics><mrow>
 <mrow>
  <msub>
   <mi>h</mi>
   <mi>i</mi>
  </msub>
  <mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub>
   <mi>h</mi>
   <mi>o</mi>
  </msub>
  <mrow><mo>(</mo>
   <mrow>
    <mfrac>
     <mrow>
      <msub>
       <mi>d</mi>
       <mi>i</mi>
      </msub>

     </mrow>
     <mrow>
      <msub>
       <mi>d</mi>
       <mi>o</mi>
      </msub>

     </mrow>
    </mfrac>

   </mrow>
  <mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo>
 </mrow>
</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow>
  <msub>
   <mi>h</mi>
   <mi>i</mi>
  </msu